{
  "compilerOptions": {
    /* Language and environment.
       `target`/`lib` are the deployment syntax floor measured in design.md
       D0.16 (iOS 15.0, macOS 12.0), whose WebKit (Safari 15) implements ES2021
       in full while leaving some ES2022 features unsupported. Nothing is ever
       emitted from here (`noEmit`), so this only decides which standard-library
       APIs the typechecker accepts; the real syntax lowering is Vite's
       (`build.target` in vite.config.ts). `useDefineForClassFields` is set
       explicitly so tsc and the bundler agree on field semantics rather than
       each falling back to a target-dependent default. */
    "target": "ES2021",
    "lib": ["ES2021", "DOM", "DOM.Iterable"],
    "jsx": "react-jsx",
    "moduleDetection": "force",
    "useDefineForClassFields": true,

    /* Modules.
       The app is ESM end to end (`package.json` has `"type": "module"`) and is
       only ever bundled, so bundler resolution mirrors how imports resolve at
       build time. `verbatimModuleSyntax` requires type-only imports to say so,
       which is what keeps per-file transpilation honest; `isolatedModules` is
       the same guarantee for the transpiler. */
    "module": "ESNext",
    "moduleResolution": "bundler",
    "resolveJsonModule": true,
    "verbatimModuleSyntax": true,
    "isolatedModules": true,
    "esModuleInterop": true,
    "noEmit": true,

    /* Types.
       `@types/node` is load-bearing: vite.config.ts reads
       `process.env.TAURI_DEV_HOST`. `vite/client` supplies `import.meta.env`
       and the asset/CSS module declarations. Both are listed explicitly so
       ambient global type packages stay opt-in; React's types arrive through
       the imports that need them.
       Accepted cost: one program covers both `src/**` and the config files, so
       listing `node` also makes Node's globals (`process`, `Buffer`, …)
       ambient inside `src/**`, where they do not exist in the webview — Vite
       substitutes only `process.env.NODE_ENV` — leaving `process.env.X` in app
       code green here and `undefined` at runtime. Closing it means either two
       configs in Vite's own template shape (app, and node/config) or dropping
       `node` from `types` and importing `node:process` in vite.config.ts. It
       is recorded rather than done, deliberately and not unnoticed: a second
       config file to keep in step against a leak no app code can hit without
       someone noticing. */
    "types": ["node", "vite/client"],
    "skipLibCheck": true,

    /* Type safety: `strict` plus the four checks below that it omits, which
       this codebase wants everywhere. */
    "strict": true,
    "noImplicitOverride": true,
    "noFallthroughCasesInSwitch": true,
    "noUnusedLocals": true,
    "noUnusedParameters": true
  },
  "include": [
    "src",
    "vite.config.ts",
    "vitest.config.ts",
    "playwright.config.ts"
  ]
}
